{
  "beruf": "buero",
  "title": "Office, admin and remote work",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "portuguese-for-work run, 2026-11-22: written by hand for Portugal (European Portuguese, pt-PT), not yet read by a human. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon, the IMT deck or another sector are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions, contracts and rules are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Office jobs in Portugal use more written Portuguese and more formal phrases on the phone. Many newcomers also work in tech, customer support and the shared-service centres of Lisbon and Porto, or remotely for companies abroad. This deck has the office and its equipment, daily tasks, meetings and deadlines, data protection and filing, and what you say on the phone, to clients and to the team. In tech and in young teams colleagues and bosses say tu; in traditional offices and with clients, start with the third person and titles (o Dr. Silva, a Eng.ª Marta).",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in an office or remotely in Portugal? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Administrativo/a",
    "Rececionista",
    "Assistente de apoio ao cliente",
    "Operador/a de call center",
    "Técnico/a de contabilidade",
    "Programador/a"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "On the phone, give the company and your name: «Imobiliária Sol, bom dia, fala a Sara.» Before you transfer a call: «Um momento, vou passar a chamada.»",
    "Formal emails start with «Exmo. Senhor Dr. Silva,» (to a woman: «Exma. Senhora Dra. Lopes,») and end with «Com os melhores cumprimentos»; with colleagues «Olá, João» and «Cumprimentos» or «Obrigado» are fine. Business Portuguese has more.",
    "Don't leave personal data on your desk or send it without need: the RGPD (the EU's GDPR) applies. Ask: «Isto pode ir por email?»",
    "Remote workers and freelancers often work as trabalhador independente (recibos verdes), with their own tax and Social Security duties. Ask an accountant. General orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
